{
  "case_id": "2008-08-192",
  "document_hash": "0a9a0c68880603e24fca1c19c16a4838ba670ca9f19ffdee4a3925eede71c8ae",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-08-192",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0a9a0c68880603e24fca1c19c16a4838ba670ca9f19ffdee4a3925eede71c8ae",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0a9a0c688806-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Can you be specific about who those persons would be? You say not a misdemeanant drug user. So who would be caught in the 843(b)?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Srinivasan",
      "case_id": "2008-08-192",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0a9a0c68880603e24fca1c19c16a4838ba670ca9f19ffdee4a3925eede71c8ae",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0a9a0c688806-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And at that time we would make the argument, just as now, a person who used the telephone in buying drugs for personal use wouldn't come within the ambit of the provision because the text of the provision goes to someone who uses a phone in committing, in facilitating, or in causing the commission of a drug felony."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2008-08-192",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0a9a0c68880603e24fca1c19c16a4838ba670ca9f19ffdee4a3925eede71c8ae",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0a9a0c688806-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 16,
        "start": 16
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: What would happen in the situation where the person who buys the drug is guilty of -- of a felony? It's an instance of felony possession. Wouldn't the application of your understanding of the buyer-seller rule in that situation lead to the conclusion that even that person could not be convicted under this statute for facilitating the commission of the felony of sale?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-08-192",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0a9a0c68880603e24fca1c19c16a4838ba670ca9f19ffdee4a3925eede71c8ae",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0a9a0c688806-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: That was changed in 1970, wasn't it? Wasn't the text \"offense\" originally, and then Congress changed it to \"felony\"?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2008-08-192",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0a9a0c68880603e24fca1c19c16a4838ba670ca9f19ffdee4a3925eede71c8ae",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0a9a0c688806-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But under the government's argument, the very same conduct by the very same person would be brought back into the fold of the statute. Even though Congress excluded it, it would be brought back into the fold of the statute by recasting it as facilitating the dealer's felony."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-08-192",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0a9a0c68880603e24fca1c19c16a4838ba670ca9f19ffdee4a3925eede71c8ae",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0a9a0c688806-0001-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF ERIC D. MILLER"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-08-192",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0a9a0c68880603e24fca1c19c16a4838ba670ca9f19ffdee4a3925eede71c8ae",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0a9a0c688806-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: And it may be that in certain States voting rights are removed, and there is on this person's record forever that he is a felon."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2008-08-192",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0a9a0c68880603e24fca1c19c16a4838ba670ca9f19ffdee4a3925eede71c8ae",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0a9a0c688806-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Is there another example in the law, anywhere in the law, where -- and there may be, I'm asking -- which you've come across, where we have an illegal business and there is a customer; and all the customer does is be a customer; and is there an example where just because he's a customer in a statutory provision that normally has a lesser penalty -- all right; imagine those circumstances -- you still can punish him as if he -- as if he ran the business?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-08-192",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0a9a0c68880603e24fca1c19c16a4838ba670ca9f19ffdee4a3925eede71c8ae",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0a9a0c688806-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: What about the -- the statement that in the manual -- maybe this is incorrect -- but that the charging policy of the Department of Justice instructs prosecutors to charge the most serious offense supported by the facts? And if that's true, then the Assistant U.S. Attorney would have no choice. The most serious offense is not misdemeanor simple possession, but it is the violation of 843(b)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Srinivasan",
      "case_id": "2008-08-192",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0a9a0c68880603e24fca1c19c16a4838ba670ca9f19ffdee4a3925eede71c8ae",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0a9a0c688806-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SRINIVASAN: Oh, no, I'm sorry, Justice Alito. If the hypothetical statute barred use of a phone in facilitating a drug felony, if it was precisely parallel to this one, then we make the same argument. But it's not because the use of a machine gun falls within the buyer/seller principle. It's because the underlying act of purchasing drugs falls within the buyer/seller principle. And if the prohibition is on use of a machine gun in some underlying act, then you have to look at the underlying act. And the underlying act is governed by the buyer/ seller principle, and buyers fall outside of it. And so the use of a machine gun by someone who is already outside of the act wouldn't bring the buyer back into the fold of the statute."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2008-08-192",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0a9a0c68880603e24fca1c19c16a4838ba670ca9f19ffdee4a3925eede71c8ae",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0a9a0c688806-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: The buyer/seller rule would apply in your view in exactly the same way."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2008-08-192",
      "confidence": 0.0,
